<Project Sdk="Snail.Sdk.Library">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>
      Shared web-client behaviour: identity and proxy rotation, per-host politeness, robots rules,
      block detection, text extraction, URL identity and the outcome shape the engines answer with.
      Knows nothing about a browser, and of an HTTP client only how to send one out through a
      configured exit — a type the framework provides, so no package reference is added for it.
    </Description>
    <PackageTags>web;scraping;search;robots;proxy;user-agent</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="HtmlAgilityPack" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The core names two of its own dependants here, which is worth explaining because it reads like the
    dependency arrow pointing backwards. It is not a reference: InternalsVisibleTo is a compile-time friend
    declaration and adds nothing to the package graph. What it buys is that Fanout and Spread — bounded
    parallelism and host interleaving — stay OUT of the public surface. Neither is part of what this toolkit
    promises: the packages answer "where is this written about?" and "what does this page say?", and how the
    work is spread across threads is machinery behind both. A type published at 0.1.0 is a promise to every
    later version, and the asymmetry decides it — public to internal is a breaking change, internal to public
    is not, so the reversible direction is the one to take before there is a consumer.

    The third name was added on 2026-09-15 for Markup, which is the same argument reaching a different type:
    the ceiling on nesting depth is not a policy a consumer tunes, so the parser stays internal, and the
    browser-backed engines read their results pages through it rather than each building a parser of its own.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Snail.Toolkit.Web.Scraping" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Snail.Toolkit.Web.Search" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Snail.Toolkit.Web.Search.Browser" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Snail.Toolkit.Web.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
